from register_common import add

add(78,'Ordinary quadratic versus nonsupersingular',[139,140,158,159,160],5520,5620,'corrected by himself later','B',
'The first definition calls a hypersurface singularity ordinary when its quadratic tangent form has nondegenerate polar form. Scans158–160 expressly correct ordinary to mean a smooth projective tangent quadric, retaining nonsupersingular for nondegenerate polar form. The cancelled multiplicity2 equivalence is false.',
'SGA7II XII1.1–1.2, printed62–64, and Conrad2014 AppendixC adopt the corrected distinction. In characteristic2 a smooth odd-rank quadric has polar radical of dimension1; x²+yz is the basic example. A cusp y²−x³ has multiplicity2 but is not ordinary.',
'SGA7II,Conrad2014','computations.py node_polar_matrix, cusp_quadratic_polar_matrix and ambient_three_char2_quadric_partials compute the quadratic parts and gradients. For x²+yz in characteristic2 the partials vanish only where y=z=0, which does not meet the projective quadric; the polar form still has radical generated by x.',group='ordinary')
add(79,'Principal parts and the erroneous zero-Hessian criterion',[141,142],5624,5701,'corrected by himself later','B',
'For a section f on smooth X/S, the zero of its first principal part gives the singular-zero scheme. The cancelled claim that the zero of the second principal part gives the supersingular locus is replaced by the determinant of its polar form.',
'A degenerate Hessian need not be zero. The corrected discriminant criterion agrees with SGA7II XII and Conrad2014 AppendixC; the quadratic form on the singular scheme is intrinsic because the lower-order terms vanish there.',
'SGA7II,Conrad2014','Proof: over characteristic not2, f=y² in A² has polar matrix diag(0,2). Its determinant is zero but its second homogeneous part is nonzero. Thus the zero of the whole quadratic form is strictly smaller than its degeneracy locus.',group='hessian')
add(80,'The Hessian on the singular scheme',[142,143],5663,5742,'proof sketched','B',
'On the singular-zero scheme of a line-bundle section, the second principal part is a canonical quadratic form in the relative cotangent module, with the line-bundle twist. Its polar determinant defines a closed nonsupersingularity obstruction compatible with arbitrary base change.',
'The modern quadratic-form treatment in Conrad2014 AppendixC and SGA7II XII gives the determinant construction. This polar determinant detects nonsupersingularity; in odd rank characteristic2 it does not detect ordinary smoothness, which instead uses the half-discriminant.',
'Conrad2014,SGA7II','Proof: changing local coordinates changes the quadratic term by the symmetric-square cotangent change when the constant and linear terms vanish. Replacing a local equation by a unit multiplies its quadratic term by that unit. Determinants transform by the square determinant character and the appropriate line-bundle power, so they glue and commute with base change.',group='hessian')
add(81,'Ramification and the polar degeneracy scheme',[143,144,145,146,147],5706,5905,'proof sketched','B',
'In the smooth X/Y/S setting, with V¹ smooth over S of the expected dimension, Proposition7 identifies the supersingular scheme with the ramification scheme of V¹→Y. The proof reduces equality of ideals to a cofactor identity for two locally direct-summand submodules P,Q of M with ranks summing to rank(M)+1.',
'This is the local discriminant/critical-locus calculation underlying SGA7II XVII’s Lefschetz theory. The supplied proof gives the diagram and explicitly leaves a sign computation; equality of zero ideals is insensitive to that unit sign. No claim of a globally fixed sign convention is made.',
'SGA7II','computations.py cofactor_sign_example verifies a3-dimensional ambient matrix case with two rank2 submodules and opposite-sign sections. Proof: top exterior powers identify the two induced maps from the common determinant line; permuting the P and Q wedge blocks changes only a sign. Their scalar coefficients therefore generate the same ideal. The exact smoothness/rank assumptions make the relevant kernels locally free.',group='ramification')
add(82,'Sheaf injection of the conormal map is insufficient',[144,145],5747,5821,'false as stated','B',
'The proof identifies smoothness of a closed subscheme with injectivity of its conormal map as a sheaf, without specifying fiberwise injectivity or a locally split injection.',
'For an lci immersion into a smooth scheme the smoothness criterion is injectivity after passage to the residue field, equivalently local splitting with locally free cokernel. A merely injective sheaf map allows singular hypersurfaces.',
'EGAIV4,SGA7II','Proof: in B=k[x,y]/(xy), the conormal map B→B² sends1 to(y,x). Its kernel is ann(x)∩ann(y)=(y)∩(x)=0, so it is sheaf-injective. At the origin its fiber map is zero, and Spec(B) is a singular node.',
refine='Use fiberwise injectivity of I/I²→Ω_X/S|W, or an equivalent locally split injection with locally free cokernel, under the finite-presentation lci hypotheses. Sheaf injectivity alone is not a smoothness test.',group='ramification')
add(83,'The determinant line of the Hessian',[147,148],5871,5942,'proof sketched','B',
'For a section of K on a smooth relative d-fold, the discriminant on the singular scheme is a section of ω_X/Y² tensor K^d. The intermediate determinant line is ω_X/Y^−2 tensor K^{−(d+1)}, rather than the uncertain K^{−2d−1} in the transcription.',
'The invariant determinant construction is standard and agrees with the line-valued quadratic convention in Conrad2014 AppendixC. The exponent mismatch is a source-reading alarm; no transcription alteration is made.',
'Conrad2014','computations.py line_bundle_determinant_exponents derives the tensor exponents in the free group of line-bundle characters from the principal-parts exact sequence and determinant-line cofactor formula. Proof: the quadratic term is in Sym²Ω tensor K; its polar map is T→Ω tensor K. Taking determinant gives (detΩ)² tensor K^d. Under f↦uf, all d rows scale by u, yielding D(uf)=u^dD(f).',group='hessian')
add(84,'Dimension threshold for normal ordinary singularities',[147,148,163],5871,5942,'corrected by himself later','B',
'The isolated nonsupersingular hypersurface claim is correct under regular-local ambient assumptions, but the early normality threshold uses the ambient dimension as if it were the hypersurface dimension. Scan163 gives the intrinsic threshold: normal when dimension≥2; reduced when dimension≥1.',
'The regular-local hypersurface is Cohen–Macaulay. A smooth projective tangent hypersurface gives an isolated singularity; Serre R1+S2 then proves normality in intrinsic dimension≥2. A one-dimensional node is a counterexample to the early off-by-one statement.',
'EGAIV2,SGA7II','Proof: A=k[x,y]_(x,y)/(xy) has a nondegenerate binary quadratic tangent form when char(k)≠2 and has dimension1, but is not normal. For an isolated hypersurface singularity of dimension≥2, every height1 localization is regular and S2 follows from Cohen–Macaulayness.',group='elementary')
add(85,'Associated graded presentation of an elementary singularity',[149,150,151,152],5947,6110,'proof sketched','B',
'For an augmented algebra with projective conormal, the ordinary quadratic condition is that its associated graded is Sym of that conormal modulo one smooth quadratic relation. In a regular-local presentation one removes m−1 independent linear equations, leaving a hypersurface whose quadratic initial form is smooth.',
'This is the standard tangent-cone characterization used in SGA7II’s ordinary-singularity setup. The general augmented-ring formulation needs the explicitly projective conormal and rank-one relation hypotheses; the regular-local Corollary10 is the clean verified case. The pages are cancelled as transcribed, without a source-given reason.',
'SGA7II,Conrad2014','Proof in the regular-local case: independent linear initial forms extend to a regular parameter system. Quotient by the first m−1 parameters. The remaining equation has order2 and smooth homogeneous initial form, so the associated graded quotient is the symmetric algebra modulo that initial form. The principal relation is regular because a nonzero polynomial over the residue field is a nonzerodivisor.',group='elementary')
add(86,'Hilbert graded ranks versus cumulative lengths',[149,150,151,162],5947,6070,'false as stated','B',
'The stated rank of gr^n for a quadratic hypersurface with d generators is binom(n+d,d)−binom(n+d−2,d), which is instead its cumulative Hilbert–Samuel length. The rank of the degree-n piece is binom(n+d−1,d−1)−binom(n+d−3,d−1).',
'The exact sequence for multiplication by a homogeneous nonzerodivisor gives the corrected Hilbert series (1−t²)/(1−t)^d. For a degree-e smooth relation replace2 by e. This resolves the later request to generalize the rank calculation without treating a finite numeric test as proof.',
'SGA7II,Conrad2014','computations.py hilbert_rank_alarm compares the two formulas and verifies the cumulative identity for a grid of d,n. Proof: 0→A[T]_(n−e)→A[T]_n→gr^n→0 gives the difference of binomial ranks. Summing degree0 through n applies the hockey-stick identity and yields the printed cumulative formula.',
refine='For d variables and one degree-e fiberwise nonzero relation, rank gr^n=binom(n+d−1,d−1)−binom(n−e+d−1,d−1), with negative-degree binomials zero. The cumulative length through degree n is binom(n+d,d)−binom(n−e+d,d).',group='hilbert')
add(87,'Fiberwise nonzero homogeneous equations and flatness',[150,151,152,153,161,162],5985,6150,'proof sketched','B',
'A homogeneous polynomial whose image is nonzero on every residue-field fiber gives injective multiplication in each degree between finite locally free symmetric powers, with locally free cokernel. The resulting graded pieces are flat and have the expected ranks. Regular complete-intersection zeros in the stated smooth relative setting are flat over the base.',
'The degreewise injection follows from field polynomial domains and the local flatness criterion; its higher-degree extension is explicitly requested and valid. Relative flatness still uses fiberwise regular sequences and finite presentation, not only a nonzero global equation.',
'Stacks056P,EGAIV4','Proof: over a residue field multiplication by a nonzero polynomial is injective. A map between finite free modules which is fiberwise injective is locally a split injection, by choosing a nonvanishing maximal minor; the cokernel is locally free. Apply degreewise and use the regular-sequence local criterion for the affine hypersurface or lci family.',group='elementary')
add(88,'Singular locus from maximal minors',[153,154],6115,6186,'corrected by himself later','B',
'For the zero scheme of a section of a rank-m vector bundle on smooth X/S, singularity is detected by the vanishing of maximal minors of E→Ω_X/S, not vanishing of the whole differential map. The source makes this correction explicitly.',
'The Jacobian/Fitting-ideal criterion proves the corrected construction and its independence under changing local generators. Whole-map vanishing would select only rank0, missing singularities of intermediate rank.',
'EGAIV4,SGA7II','Proof: the cokernel of the Jacobian is Ω_V/S. Failure of the expected rank m is the vanishing of ∧^m of the differential, hence the ideal of m×m minors. For m>1 matrices of rank m−1 are singular points even though the map is nonzero.',group='intrinsic')
add(89,'The constant-rank quadratic obstruction',[154,155,156,166,167],6153,6250,'proof sketched','C',
'On the rank-(m−1) stratum of the Jacobian, K is its rank-one kernel and W its locally free cokernel of rank d−m+1. The quadratic obstruction K→Sym²W has polar determinant in K^{−(d−m+1)} tensor(det W)². The supersingular set also includes the deeper rank-drop locus V″.',
'The local lci Hessian construction is established. The supplied generalization of Proposition7 is left for verification on156 and partly worked out on166–167; two local conormal identifications and a convention-dependent sign remain unverified on the pages. The exact intrinsic scheme statement is treated separately below.',
'SGA7II,Conrad2014','Proof of the displayed line: a form in K^−1 tensor Sym²W gives W^vee→K^−1 tensor W. Determinant yields K^−rank(W) tensor(det W)². The constant-rank hypotheses split the Jacobian locally and ensure both K and W are locally free. This proves the line formula, not every asserted global identification.',confidence='medium',group='intrinsic')
add(90,'Constant fiber rank is not a scheme-theoretic rank condition',[155,166,169],6189,6230,'corrected by himself later','B',
'Scan154 identifies an invertible direct-summand kernel with a fiber-rank condition; scan166 expressly corrects that earlier equivalence on page15 line10. The separate constant-rank lemma on scan155 already assumes that the exterior-power map vanishes as a morphism, not merely at residue-field points, and is valid with its remaining rank hypothesis. In the later conormal presentation on scan169, local freeness of the cokernel and of the target implies flatness of the image and hence arbitrary kernel base change.',
'Under vanishing of the next minors and invertibility of one maximal surviving minor, matrix row reduction gives a split constant-rank presentation; then kernel, image and cokernel are locally free and commute with all base change. The nilpotent-base distinction is essential.',
'EGAIV4,SGA7II','Proof: multiplication by ε on A=k[ε]/(ε²) is zero on its unique residue field but is not the zero map. In contrast, if a rank-r minor is a unit, row/column reduction puts a matrix in block form diag(I_r,B); vanishing of all(r+1)-minors forces B=0 as a matrix over A.',group='intrinsic')
add(91,'The determinantal codimension inequality is reversed',[156],6231,6250,'false as stated','B',
'A footnote asserts that the rank-drop locus of an arbitrary matrix has codimension at least d−m+1, even over a regular base.',
'The general determinantal height theorem is an upper bound on codimension; equality needs a suitable generic/transversality condition. A zero matrix over any regular affine space gives the whole base as rank-drop locus and refutes the printed lower bound.',
'EGAIV4,SGA7II','Proof: choose m≤d and the zero map O^m→O^d on A^N. Every maximal minor is0, so the degeneracy locus has codimension0<d−m+1. All the stated regularity assumptions on the ambient base hold.',
refine='The ideal of maximal minors has height at most d−m+1 when the expected bound is applicable; equality or a lower bound requires independent genericity hypotheses. No such hypothesis is supplied by regularity of the base alone.',group='intrinsic')
add(92,'Generic inseparable degree of the conormal map',[158,159,160],6262,6370,'open on the pages','C',
'After withdrawing separable duality in characteristic2 and odd dimension, the author tentatively suggests inseparable degree exactly2, explicitly without guarantee. The supplied passage does not unambiguously distinguish an arbitrary original embedding from a sufficiently high Veronese embedding.',
'SGA7II XVII treats the generic quadratic tangency after a sufficiently ample embedding; general Gauss/conormal maps in positive characteristic have more varied behavior. The exact scope of this tentative degree2 assertion remains a reading-and-hypothesis problem, not an accepted universal theorem.',
'SGA7II,Conrad2014',confidence='medium',group='duality_correction')
add(93,'Smooth homogeneous forms of arbitrary degree',[160,161],6338,6406,'proof sketched','B',
'A degree-e form on a finite locally free module is smooth if it is nonzero on every fiber and its projective zero scheme is smooth over the base. This is detected after arbitrary surjective base change and defines an open locus. The Jacobian criterion uses the ideal generated by the form and all partial derivatives.',
'The smoothness criterion and properness of projective space give openness. Euler’s formula permits dropping the form only when e is invertible; retaining it makes the statement valid in every characteristic. SGA7II XII supplies the quadratic special case, and the general relative Jacobian criterion is EGA IV.',
'EGAIV4,SGA7II','Proof: the nonsmooth locus of the projective hypersurface is closed in a proper parameter-family fiber, so its image is closed. Fiberwise smoothness plus the fiberwise nonzero Cartier condition gives smoothness. On an affine chart the Jacobian ideal is generated by f and its derivatives; homogeneous compatibility gives the irrelevant-ideal power criterion.',group='elementary')
add(94,'Higher-degree elementary singularities and their local properties',[161,162,163],6375,6490,'proof sketched','B',
'The proposed degree-e elementary singularity has tangent cone a single smooth degree-e hypersurface. The notes request the higher-degree Hilbert/presentation proofs and state that the local ring is a hypersurface complete intersection, Cohen–Macaulay and isolated; it is normal in dimension≥2 and reduced in dimension≥1. In dimension0 the model is a DVR quotient by a power of a parameter.',
'The Hilbert calculation is proved in C-SA23-hyp-086. The regular-local presentation and smooth projective tangent cone give the other local properties; the promised blow-up proof is absent from these pages. The abstract local formulation requires the stated noetherian/regular-presentation hypotheses or a completion argument.',
'EGAIV2,EGAIV4,SGA7II','Proof: remove independent linear equations in a regular ambient ring, obtaining one equation whose initial homogeneous form is smooth. In the blow-up, the exceptional hypersurface is smooth and the strict transform is regular along it by the chart Jacobian criterion; away from the closed point there are no nearby singularities. Hypersurface CM plus isolated singularity gives S2 and R1 in dimension≥2. A one-dimensional reduced tangent cone yields reducedness; dimension0 is the stated principal Artin quotient.',group='elementary')
add(95,'Geometric elementary singularities and residue fields',[163,164],6454,6526,'false as stated','B',
'The finite-separable-residue statement compares absolute and geometric elementary singularities at closed points. The author asks whether finite can be removed. For an arbitrary nonclosed point with separably generated residue field, that strengthening is false.',
'Closed finite separable residue extensions do not introduce extra tangent directions, so the standard comparison holds. At a nonclosed point, base change to its residue field followed by the rational geometric point changes the local embedding dimension; the proposed strengthening fails.',
'EGAIV4,SGA7II','Proof: let X=Spec k[x,y,t]/(xy) and x0=(x,y), with κ(x0)=k(t), separably generated over k. O_X,x0=k(t)[x,y]_(x,y)/(xy) is an ordinary node. After K=k(t) base change, the K-rational point t=t0 has local tangent cone K[x,y,w]/(xy), where w=t−t0. Its projective quadric is singular at[0:0:1], and the singular locus is not isolated. Thus geometric ordinaryness at that rational point fails.',
refine='Retain the closed-point finite separable residue-extension hypothesis in this absolute/geometric comparison. For nonclosed points distinguish the local ring at the original point from the local ring at the rational point after residue-field base change.',group='elementary')
add(96,'Faint duplicate of the conormal calculation',[165],6532,6569,'stated without proof','F',
'The faint duplicate records only fragments of the generalized Proposition7 argument. Its exact maps and ranks cannot be identified from the supplied transcription.',
'The clearer scans166–167 are a separate witness and support the statements recorded there; their content is not inserted into the faint text. No literature theorem determines the missing literal reading.',
'SGA7II',confidence='low',group='intrinsic')
add(97,'Intrinsic supersingular scheme and generator independence',[168,169,170,171],6658,6807,'open on the pages','C',
'The rank-drop loci V¹ and V″ are intrinsic by exterior powers. The author explicitly leaves unverified the generator independence of V² and the factorization K→ker(N→Ω)→Sym²W needed for its intrinsic quadratic obstruction. The proposed open nondegenerate locus is also accidentally called the closed V².',
'The cotangent-complex and lci Hessian viewpoint supplies an intrinsic modern framework, and SGA7II uses the ordinary-singularity consequence. No exact source was found proving this draft’s entire scheme-valued factorization with its stated arbitrary-base hypotheses. The remaining task is that precise construction, not basic Jacobian invariance.',
'SGA7II,Conrad2014,EGAIV4','Targeted check: changes of generators preserve the Fitting ideals defining V¹ and V″. On the split rank stratum the conormal image is flat, which repairs the kernel/base-change argument. The second-order factorization and equality of the claimed closed subschemes have not been independently proved in this run.',confidence='medium',group='intrinsic')
add(98,'Failure of transversality for a smooth pair',[171,172],6773,6847,'open on the pages','C',
'The final generalization takes a smooth E/X with a smooth subscheme E0/X and a section, and proposes ordinary or supersingular failure-of-transversality loci. It asks whether E0 must be étale over X and promises further notes.',
'The local reduction to a regular immersion and its conormal map is standard. The supplied notes do not complete the definition or specify all ranks/base-change hypotheses; targeted searches in EGA/SGA and quadratic-discriminant references did not identify the exact general statement. The residual is the intrinsic second-order construction for this pair.',
'EGAIV4,SGA7II,Conrad2014',confidence='medium',group='transversality')
